  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Accelerating Peri-Urban Water and Sanitation Services in Kasai Oriental and Lomami provinces (DRC WASH)</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">(Democratic Republic of the Congo):  .USAID&apos;s Accelerating Peri-Urban Water and Sanitation Services in Kasai Oriental and Lomami Provinces Activity aims to increase access to basic water services for 550,000 people and sanitation services for 50,000 people through improved governance and strengthened service provider models. In collaboration with provincial and local entities, FY 2022 funds will support in-kind grant assistance for twenty-nine autonomous Water Service Providers (WSPs). These grants will support the WSPs to accelerate and improve access to drinking water in their respective geographic areas, accelerating the Congolese population&apos;s level of service along the water ladder and USAID&apos;s Localization Agenda..With FY 2022 funds, grant activities will multiply the number of autonomous WSPs and private sector entities reporting increased recognition of rights by national, provincial, and local leadership and public sector entities and increase the percentage of targeted WSPs exhibiting increased institutional capacity. Through this activity, USAID will strengthen institutional governance in support of water service delivery across all levels of the service ladder and in all technical areas, improve administrative management of the water sector, increase knowledge of the legal and regulatory framework for water resource governance, and promote gender-sensitive water management...This activity will contribute to Mission Objective 4.1 (Strengthen institutional and community capacity to prevent outbreaks from becoming pandemics as well as respond to and overcome frequent public health and humanitarian emergencies) by supporting sustainable water and sanitation systems. Clean water and sanitation prevents many infectious diseases from spreading and promotes healthier, more stable, and productive communities, thereby reducing threats to U.S. national security. This activity advances the U.S. Global Water Strategy, which defines water security as a national security</narrative>
  </description>
